change no contract, and this one changes none: the single added file is a note, and the head carries no code. Preserved.\n\nSECURITY. The boundary the note proposes moving is review independence, from the gs review validator into the fold, and moving it is a strengthening rather than a risk. A check enforced only in cmd/gs binds only actors who use that CLI, while records can be appended through other write paths; the fold is the authority every writer passes through. The same is true of submit's check 3, which converts today's silent discovery rule in latestCompletion — an artifact by the wrong author is simply not picked up — into a typed refusal at append time. On undertake, requester and performer are the same actor, so its ratification arm checks nothing; the note states this plainly and correctly leaves the real checks with the review approval and the merge receipt, both signed by other actors. No unresolved security defect.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. No cut requested. The length is carried by the six conditions and by evidence rather than prose, and the note twice declines to claim more than it delivers.\n\nONE NON-BLOCKING OBSERVATION, for the adoption proposal rather than this head. The note says only two places test the compiled name artifact, and names validateReview in cmd/gs/main.go and the successor publisher in cmd/gs/succession.go. Both are correct. A third site hardcodes workroom.KindArtifact — internal/perfscenario/fixture.go:286, which writes a synthetic artifact. The claim as written says \"in the tooling\", and a fixture generator is not tooling, so the sentence is defensible; but since the note says that asymmetry decides most of the migration story, whoever writes the migration should count that site too.\n\nNo change to this head is required. Any change to it invalidates this approval.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:cf4739f2d5104a1d13733c8787934cff6a301eb4","head":"a63f0953c2f52e965406c5cbd682cee17a365